AGENT DECISION CASES: EVIDENCE BEFORE ACTION

CASE A: 'Why did the program not start?'
  ps
  cat /proc/memory
  ls /tmp
  hexdump /tmp/a.out 0 32
Look for unavailable process slots, missing/oversized images, and
build errors. Do not repeatedly spawn on a full process table.

CASE B: 'Why is a file read failing?'
  ls /tmp
  lsof
  trace on -s
  cat /tmp/KNOWN
  trace show
  trace on
'KNOWN' is a placeholder for an existing file, not a command.
Compare open/ and read/ return values. Trace capture has side effects
on the global tracing state; restore the prior mode as appropriate.

CASE C: 'Which device is currently controlling the display?'
  pins
  fbctl status
  ps
Inspect only. Do not issue fbctl off or kill without authorization.

CASE D: 'Why did a network MCP call fail?'
  ps
  lsof
  cat /proc/net/memory
  cat /proc/net/wifi
Distinguish transport failure from command failure. Before retrying
a write, check state and whether the first operation took effect.

CASE E: 'Explain a suspicious memory location'
  cat /proc/memory
  mem -s kernel
  hexdump /dev/mem VERIFIED_ADDRESS 32
VERIFIED_ADDRESS must be replaced with a confirmed mapped address.
Never invent addresses or write arbitrary memory.

RULES FOR REPORTING
  Separate observed output from inference. Preserve small excerpts,
  include command names and PID only if actually observed. Respect
  3-second execution and 4096-byte output limits. Avoid interactive
  or continuous commands. If hardware may be damaged, ask first.
